.TH CLOG_OPEN_FILE_MP 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_open_file_mp, clog_open_file \- create a file-backed logger with optional log rotation
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_open_file_mp(const char *path, clog_level_t min_level,
                    const clog_rotation_cfg_t *cfg,
                    const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline clog clog_open_file(const char *path,
                    clog_level_t min_level,
                    const clog_rotation_cfg_t *cfg,
                    const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to the file at path. The function
creates the file with mode 0644, which the umask of the process
narrows, when it does not exist.  It opens the
file for append when it does exist.  For a file that already exists,
the function finds the current size.  It asks fstat(2) first, and it
falls back to an lseek(2) to the end of the file.  The thresholds for
size rotation are then correct for an append to a log that already
exists.  The function can fail to find that size by either method.
When cfg->size_rotation_enabled is true, the call then fails and
returns CLOG_INVALID.  It does not silently start the byte counter
from zero.  Such a start would let the file grow well past
cfg->max_file_size before its first rotation.

Log rotation is on only when cfg is not NULL.  You can turn size
rotation and time rotation on separately.

When rotation is on, the logger opens the directory of path and keeps
it open for its whole life.  It opens the file, and does every rename,
listing, compression and deletion of a rotation, relative to that
directory.  A relative path therefore keeps naming the same file after
the process changes its working directory with chdir(2).

Size rotation triggers after the write that takes the total bytes
written to cfg->max_file_size or past it.  The library checks it after
each write.

Time rotation triggers on the first write after
cfg->rotation_interval_us microseconds pass.  The check runs on whole
seconds, so an interval that is not a whole number of seconds rotates
at the first whole second at or after its end.  That time counts from the
last rotation.  Before the first rotation of the logger, when the file
already holds data, it counts from the moment at which that file
began.  That moment is the stamp of the newest rotated generation on
disk, because the rotation that made it also created the current
file.  Without one, it is the birth time of the file, where statx(2)
reports one, and the last modification time otherwise.  When the file
is empty or new, the interval counts from the creation of the logger.
A process that runs for less than the interval, or restarts often,
therefore still rotates.  A starting moment in the future counts as
the creation of the logger.  The library checks the interval before
each write.

When a rotation fires, the library first creates the next live file
under a private name, <path>.tmp followed by 11 random hex digits,
with O_CREAT, O_EXCL and O_NOFOLLOW, so the file is always a new
regular file that the library made.  It then renames the current file
to <path>.<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS>, a rename that never replaces an entry that
already has that name, and renames the new file onto <path>.  Both
renames are atomic.  The rotation never opens <path> or a rotated name.
A link, a FIFO or a file that another user puts at <path> while a
rotation runs is replaced by the new live file and never receives a
record, and never blocks the rotation.  A process that stops between
the creation of the private file and its rename leaves it behind
empty; the next rotating open of path removes such an empty regular
file, and nothing else at a name of that shape.  A second rotation
inside the same second takes a collision suffix, which runs _0001,
_0002 and so on.  The suffix is zero-padded to four digits, and a
number above 9999 takes the digits that it needs, such as _10000.
Every rotation takes a name above every rotated name before it.  A
later rotation inside the same second takes the next number, and never
a name that a deletion freed.  A logger that starts in a directory
which already holds rotated generations (see below) numbers its
rotations above the newest of them, so a process that restarts inside
the same second continues the sequence.  When the clock reads an
earlier second than the newest rotated name, the rotation keeps the
stamp of that name and takes the next number.  The new live file gets
the permission bits of the file that it replaces, whatever the umask,
and the owner and the group of that file where the process may give
them, as logrotate(8) does.  When the process cannot give it the
group, the library clears the group bits, so that no other group gains
access.  A log that its operator restricted, to 0600 or
0640 for example, therefore stays restricted in every generation, and
each \&.gz file takes the bits of its source.  Once the new live file
holds <path>, the library deletes the oldest rotated files past
cfg->max_rotated_files.  A rotation that fails never deletes a rotated
file on its own account.

A directory entry is a rotated generation of the log only when its
name matches <path>.<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS>[_N][.gz] exactly, where N is four
zero-padded digits or a number from 10000 to 999999999, and the entry,
examined without following a link, is a regular file whose owner is
the effective user of the process or the owner of the live file, and
whose timestamp lies no more than 30 days above both the clock and the
newest name that the logger made.  The naming of rotations, the
deletion, the compression and the recovery of compressions act on
generations alone.  Every other entry is never opened, compressed,
renamed or deleted, and takes no place among the
cfg->max_rotated_files kept.  That covers an unrelated file whose name
starts with a timestamp of a similar shape, a link, a FIFO, a file
that another user owns, and a name stamped far in the future, so
another user who can write the directory cannot make the logger
write, read, archive or delete a file through a link, block on a
FIFO, or stop its rotations.  An entry that matches the name and is
not a generation gets a "log rotation: ignored an entry that is not a
rotated file of this log: <name>" record at CLOG_WARN in the live
file, with every character outside printable ASCII, every quote and
every backslash written as '?'.  At most 8 such records go out for a
log in any minute.

When path itself is a symbolic link, the logger writes through the
link until the first rotation, which moves the link to a rotated name.
From then on the live file is a regular file that the logger made, and
the link, which is not a generation, stays where the rotation put it.

The timestamp and the collision number of each rotated name decide
which rotated files are the oldest.  The library orders the names by
the timestamp and then by the number, as a number.  Because every
rotation takes a name above every name before it, that order is the
order of the rotations, and the deletion always keeps the newest
generations.
The library does not use the modification time of a rotated file.
The compression step writes a compressed rotated file, so its
modification time records when that compression finished.  It does not
record how old the log content inside it is.

The prune pass counts generations.  A generation is one rotation.
The uncompressed file and the \&.gz file of one generation count
once.  The prune pass of a rotation
never deletes the generation that the same rotation has just created,
and still counts it against cfg->max_rotated_files.  A prune pass never
deletes the generation whose compression is running, and still counts
it.  A pass keeps the newest cfg->max_rotated_files generations and
deletes every older one, except an older one whose compression is
running, which the library deletes as soon as that compression
ends.  A generation that still waits for its compression is deleted
like any other once it is older than the newest
cfg->max_rotated_files.  At most cfg->max_rotated_files generations
plus the one under compression therefore stay on the disk.

When cfg->compress_rotated is true, zlib gzip-compresses each rotated
file after the rotation.  The library writes the compressed file to
<rotated-name>.gz.  After a success, it removes the original
uncompressed file.  A compression thread of the logger does the work.
The first rotation that needs this thread starts it.  The thread
compresses one file at a time, the oldest file first.

The rotation only puts the file in a queue.  Therefore, the thread
that logs and the async writer thread never wait for gzip.  A producer
behind a bounded async queue also never waits for gzip.  The last
clog_close(3) of the file waits until all queued compressions are
complete.  A ccol_log_fatal(3) record waits for them within the budget
of the exit drain below.

The directory can be unable to hold the name <rotated-name>.gz.tmp
(NAME_MAX, 255 bytes on common filesystems).  Then no compression of
that file can succeed.  The file stays uncompressed.  The library
writes a "log rotation: a rotated file stays uncompressed: ..." record
at CLOG_WARN into the new live file.

The standard gunzip(1) and zcat(1) tools can read the \&.gz files that
the library makes.  A program that links the static library must also
link with -lz.  pkg-config --static --libs ccollections adds -lz.  The
shared library has zlib as its own dependency.

A compression opens its source with O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK, and
reads it only when the descriptor is a regular file that the effective
user or the owner of the live file owns, so it never reads through a
link and never waits on a FIFO or a device.
A compression writes its output to <rotated-name>.gz.tmp, flushes it
to the disk, and only then gives it the name <rotated-name>.gz and
removes the uncompressed file.  A \&.gz file therefore always holds a
complete gzip stream, even when the process stops in the middle of a
compression.  The prune pass and the naming of rotations never look
at the \&.tmp file.  A filesystem whose fsync(2) answers EINVAL or
ENOSYS offers no flush to disk, and the compression goes ahead without
it.  A compression never replaces a file that already
has the \&.gz name.  The \&.gz file gets the permission bits of the
file that it compresses, as gzip(1) gives them, and its owner and
group under the same rule as the new live file of a rotation.

When cfg->compress_rotated is true, the open finishes what an earlier
process left unfinished in the directory, for generations alone.  It
removes every <rotated-name>.gz.tmp file of path that passes the same
test of type, owner and timestamp.  A \&.gz file beside its own uncompressed file is
not known to be complete, and the uncompressed file holds everything,
so the open removes that \&.gz file.  It then queues every uncompressed
generation of path for compression, the oldest first.  A generation
whose \&.gz name holds an entry that is not a generation stays
uncompressed.

A process that ends with exit(3), or a return from main(), while the
logger is still open drains it within a budget of 5 seconds for the
whole process: an exit handler that the library registers when it
loads delivers what an async logger still holds, as a
ccol_log_fatal(3) record does, and waits for the queued compressions.
The budget bounds every lock that the drain takes too.  A logger whose
lock another thread holds past the budget is skipped, and the records
that it still holds can be lost.  Every write of the drain is bounded
too, on a pipe, a socket and a terminal as well; see
clog_open_fd_mp(3).  A
compression that the budget cuts off stops, removes its \&.tmp output
and keeps the uncompressed file, and the next open of path compresses
it.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no exit handler.

One file has one backing store in a process whenever a logger of it
rotates.  When a rotating logger of this process already writes the
file, or when this call rotates and a logger of this process already
writes the file, the call joins that logger, exactly as
clog_derive(3) does.  The new handle has its own min_level and its
own fields.  It shares the output target, the format, the rotation
and the async writer thread.  The file is the same when the directory
and the last component of path are the same, whatever spelling of the
path each call uses.  The call returns CLOG_INVALID instead when its
rotation configuration, its async configuration (NULL or not, and
every resolved value) or its allocator differs from the one of that
logger, and also when one of the two rotates and the other does not.
Two independent backing stores on one rotating file would each
rename, delete and compress files that the other one still writes,
and lose records.  Two opens of one file that both have rotation off
stay two independent loggers, and each one appends whole records to
the file.  A call for a file whose last clog_close(3) still runs
waits until that close has finished.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
path       Destination log file path. Copied internally.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped.
cfg        The rotation configuration, or NULL to turn rotation off.
           A max_file_size or max_rotated_files <= 0, and a
           rotation_interval_us of 0, fall back to their respective
           CLOG_DEFAULT_* constant:
             size_rotation_enabled  - turns size rotation on
             max_file_size          - int64_t bytes threshold
                                      (<= 0 = 10 MiB)
             time_rotation_enabled  - turns time rotation on
             rotation_interval_us   - uint64_t microseconds
                                      (0 = 86400000000 = 1 day)
             max_rotated_files      - files kept (<= 0 = 7)
             compress_rotated       - gzip-compress each rotated file
           max_file_size is a fixed-width int64_t.
           rotation_interval_us is a uint64_t.  This struct therefore
           has one layout for each target.  This is true for all
           values of _FILE_OFFSET_BITS and _TIME_BITS in the
           including translation unit.  The caller needs no
           particular compilation flag for the struct to mean the
           same thing on both sides of the library boundary.
async_cfg  The configuration for async logging, or NULL for
           synchronous logging.  When it is not NULL, a log_*() call
           formats its own message alone.  It hands the rest to a
           dedicated writer thread.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3) for the
           full field list.  A struct of all zeros still turns async
           mode on, which differs from cfg above.  Every zero field
           falls back to its own default.  Only a literal NULL keeps
           the logging synchronous.
mprocs     A custom allocator, which holds function pointers for
           malloc, free, calloc and realloc.  Pass NULL to use the
           default system allocator.  All four pointers must be set
           when mprocs is not NULL.  A struct that is only part
           filled makes the call return CLOG_INVALID, and it creates
           no logger.  The library copies the struct internally.  The
           caller can free it after this call.
           Every logger that clog_derive() makes from this one
           inherits the allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new logger handle on success.
Returns CLOG_INVALID after a failure.  A bad path, a permission error,
an mprocs that is not valid, and a failed allocation all cause one.  So
does size rotation that is on when the function cannot find the current
size of a file that already exists.  So does a file that a rotating
logger of this process already writes with another configuration, or
a rotating open of a file that a logger of this process without
rotation writes.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The logger owns the file descriptor and closes it on clog_close().
O_CLOEXEC is set on the file descriptor.
The library does the rename and the open of the new fd inside the
locked section.  No log line is therefore lost, and no log line falls
between others across the rotation boundary.
When compress_rotated is true, the gzip compression runs on the
compression thread of the logger, after the new fd is already in use,
and outside the mutex.  The I/O of the compression then stalls no log
writer.
The directory descriptor of a rotating logger carries O_CLOEXEC, as
the file descriptor does.
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with clogger:
  - Call fork(2) before you open a logger.
  - Open a logger, call fork(2), and then call exec(3) immediately.
A process that continues with no exec(3) while a handle from before
the fork is live does not use a supported pattern.

The library also gives a defense in depth.  A handle that is open at
the time of such a fork is technically usable in the child.  Log,
derive and close operations on that handle work correctly on both
sides.  The async writer thread of a logger does not exist in a forked
child.  In the child, an inherited handle with async mode on changes
to synchronous logging.  This change is transparent.

This fallback is part of the fork protection of the library.  A build
with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 does not include it.  Such a process
must not fork while it holds a live async logger.

The compression thread also does not exist in the child.  The
compressions that the parent put in its queue stay with the parent.  A
later rotation in the child starts its own compression thread.

When async_cfg is not NULL, a batch that the writer thread builds can
cover several records that the application logged moments apart.  Such
a batch can cross a rotation boundary.  The library writes it to
whichever file is current at the time of the flush.  It does not split
the batch exactly at the moment at which each record was logged.

The kernel can accept only part of a write.  A filesystem that has
just run out of space does this, and so does an RLIMIT_FSIZE ceiling.
The bytes that it did take then sit on the disk with no terminating
newline for that record.  A synchronous logger writes that newline
itself.  The next record therefore starts a line of its own, and
nothing joins it onto the fragment.  The logger then writes a "log
record truncated: <n> of <m> bytes written" record that names the
loss.  An async logger keeps the bytes that the write did not take in
its batching buffer.  It offers them again on the next flush.  They
are the exact continuation of what did reach the disk.  Once the
condition clears, the logger therefore completes the record and
delivers every record behind it.  Both size rotation and time rotation
stand down while those bytes are outstanding.  The two halves of a
record therefore always reach the same file.  The short write makes a
rotation due, and the flush that finally delivers those bytes takes
it.  Until then the current file can sit past cfg->max_file_size by
the rest of one batch.

A flush keeps the undelivered bytes only after an error that a later
attempt can outlast: ENOSPC, EDQUOT, EFBIG, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM, or EAGAIN
or EINTR that ends a wait.  Any other write error, such as EPIPE,
ECONNRESET or EBADF, fails the same way on every retry.  The flush
then drops the undelivered bytes and writes a "log record truncated:
undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>"
record in their place, and the records after it go out as usual.  The
last flush of clog_close(3) has no later retry, so what it cannot
deliver is named the same way, with "the logger closed" as the reason.

A record can have to reach the file before those bytes do.  The
library never writes such a record between the two halves of the
record that those bytes belong to.  There are two such cases.  One is
a CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG record, which the library never batches.  The other
is a record that takes the synchronous write path.  Each one first
gives the outstanding bytes one more attempt at delivery of its own.
When the file still will not take them, it gives up on them, and on
every remaining line of the split record, backtrace lines included.  It
then writes the missing newline of the split record, when a line is
open, and a "log record truncated: <n> undelivered bytes dropped"
record in their place.  A logfmt record whose backtrace lines are still
outstanding counts as split even when the bytes already on the disk end
with the newline of its first line.  The
library keeps the records that the batch holds behind the split one.
A later flush delivers them, so they follow the record that it had to
write past them.  To give up on a continuation leaves nothing
outstanding.  The next check therefore takes the rotation that stood
down for it, exactly as it would after a delivery.

A write to a regular file cannot raise SIGPIPE, and a logger on one
never touches the disposition of SIGPIPE or any signal mask.  When
path names a FIFO, the logger treats it as a pipe; see
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3).
.fi
